{{nihongo|'''Hirogawa Prefecture'''|広川県|Hirogawa Ken}} is a coastal [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located on the southern shore of [[Harujima]] Island, facing the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]]. Its population as of 2026 was estimated at 749,893. It is the 35th largest prefecture by land area with an area of 2,959.74 square kilometres. Its capital city is [[Hirogawa, Hirogawa|Hirogawa]]. Hirogawa Prefecture is renowned as Okaiken's premier source of {{nihongo|'''iwashi'''|鰯}}—the Pacific sardine harvested from the nutrient-rich currents of the Sea of Mitoyagi—and for its heavy industrial base in cement, chemicals, and shipbuilding. The prefecture is often referred to as the {{nihongo|"Silver Gate of the Sea"|海の銀門|Umi no Ginmon}} for the shimmering schools of sardines that have defined its economy and cuisine for centuries.

==History==
[[File:Hirogawa Harbor 16th Century.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The harbor of Hirogawa in the 16th century, showing the bustling fishing settlement and early sardine processing operations.]]

===The Strait Guardians===
Prior to the [[Saito Restoration]], the coastal territory now known as Hirogawa was governed by the {{nihongo|Hirogawa Clan|広川氏|Hirogawa-shi}}, a maritime lord lineage that controlled the vital strait passages connecting the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] to the open [[Shendan Ocean]]. Unlike the inland domains that relied on agricultural taxation, the Hirogawa lords built their wealth on the massive iwashi runs that seasonal currents drove through the narrow straits each autumn, as well as on tolls extracted from merchant vessels navigating the treacherous coastal channels.

The clan's stronghold, {{nihongo|Hirogawa Castle|広川城|Hirogawa-jō}}, was constructed in 1412 on a rocky headland overlooking the main strait. The fortress commanded a panoramic view of the shipping lanes, allowing the Hirogawa lords to monitor and regulate all maritime traffic between the Sea of Mitoyagi and the southern trade routes. During the [[Sengoku period]], the Hirogawa Clan maintained a pragmatic neutrality, offering safe harbor to all belligerent fleets while developing the sardine processing techniques that would later make the prefecture famous.

===The Iwashi Boom===
[[File:Hirogawa Iwashi Processing 1890.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Sardine processing facilities along the Hirogawa waterfront in the 1890s, during the height of the iwashi boom.]]

The transformation of Hirogawa into Okaiken's sardine capital began in earnest following the [[Saito Restoration]]. The construction of the coastal railway in 1878 connected the prefecture's fishing ports to the metropolitan markets of [[Okami]], enabling the rapid transport of fresh iwashi to urban consumers. More significantly, the railway facilitated the shipment of dried and fermented sardine products—{{nihongo|'''niboshi'''|煮干し}} (dried sardines for stock) and {{nihongo|'''shio-kara'''|塩辛}} (salted sardine guts)—which became essential pantry staples across Okaiken.

The {{nihongo|'''Great Iwashi Boom'''|鰯景気|Iwashi Keiki}} of the 1880s and 1890s transformed Hirogawa from a modest fishing domain into a major economic center. The prefecture's processing facilities expanded dramatically, and the influx of fishing wealth funded the construction of grand civic buildings, schools, and harbor infrastructure. The sardine became so central to the regional identity that the Hirogawa city crest incorporated a stylized iwashi—a symbol that persists on the prefectural flag to this day.

===The Industrial Corridor===
The early 20th century brought a profound industrial diversification to Hirogawa. The prefecture's coastal geography—deep-water harbors, flat coastal plains, and proximity to the capital—made it an ideal location for heavy industrial development. The {{nihongo|'''Hirogawa Industrial Belt'''|広川工業地帯|Hirogawa Kōgyō Chitai}} was established along the coastline between Hirogawa city and [[Otaira]], attracting major cement, chemical, and steel manufacturers drawn by the excellent harbor facilities and the abundant limestone deposits in the surrounding hills.

The {{nihongo|'''Hirogawa Cement Works'''|広川セメント工場|Hirogawa Semento Kōjō}}, founded in 1912, became one of the largest cement producers in Harujima, supplying the construction materials for Okami's rapid urban expansion. Chemical manufacturers followed, producing industrial solvents, fertilizers, and petrochemical products. The shipbuilding industry, rooted in the prefecture's maritime heritage, also expanded significantly, with the {{nihongo|'''Otaira Shipyard'''|大平造船所|Otaira Zōsensho}} constructing commercial vessels for the Shendan Ocean trade routes.

===Modern Development===
The completion of the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] through Hirogawa in 1967 cemented the prefecture's dual identity as both an industrial powerhouse and a commuter hub for the capital region. The {{nihongo|Hirogawa Station|広川駅|Hirogawa-eki}} became a critical junction on the high-speed rail network, enabling rapid freight movement for industrial products while supporting daily commuter flows to Okami.

Today, Hirogawa Prefecture balances its heavy industrial base with its enduring maritime traditions, maintaining the largest iwashi landing port in the Sea of Mitoyagi while continuing to innovate in sustainable chemistry and advanced shipbuilding.

==Geography==
[[File:Hirogawa Coastline.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The rugged coastline near Shiosaki, showing the deep-water harbors that have sustained the prefecture's fishing and shipping industries.]]

Hirogawa Prefecture occupies a coastal region on the southern shore of Harujima Island, facing the Sea of Mitoyagi. The prefecture's coastline extends for approximately 180 kilometers along the strait, characterized by numerous deep-water bays, rocky headlands, and natural harbors that have supported the region's maritime economy since antiquity. The {{nihongo|'''Hirogawa Strait'''|広川海峡|Hirogawa Kaikyō}}, a narrow passage connecting the Sea of Mitoyagi to the Shendan Ocean, is the prefecture's defining geographic feature and has shaped its history as a maritime gateway.

The coastal plains are relatively narrow, rising quickly into the {{nihongo|'''Hirogawa Hills'''|広川丘陵|Hirogawa Kyūryō}}, a range of low, forested hills that contain the limestone deposits essential to the cement industry. The {{nihongo|'''Tsukumo River'''|九十九川|Tsukumo-gawa}} and its tributaries water the central plain, providing freshwater for both the fishing industry's processing operations and the industrial corridor's manufacturing needs.

The prefecture includes several offshore islands, the most significant being {{nihongo|'''Ukishima'''|浮島}} and {{nihongo|'''Okushima'''|沖島}} off the eastern coast. These islands maintain traditional fishing communities and serve as waystations for vessels navigating the strait.

