==Economy==
[[File:Hirogawa Iwashi Market.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Hirogawa Central Fish Market during the morning iwashi auction, the largest sardine market in the Sea of Mitoyagi.]]

Hirogawa Prefecture possesses a diversified economy centered on sardine fishing, heavy industry, and its role as a maritime gateway between the Sea of Mitoyagi and the Shendan Ocean. The prefecture's coastal geography has shaped its economic identity since antiquity, while its industrial corridor has driven modern development.

===Iwashi Fisheries===
The maritime sector remains the cultural and economic foundation of Hirogawa Prefecture. The deep-water bays and strait passages support Okaiken's largest iwashi fishing fleet, with over 1,400 registered vessels operating from ports in Hirogawa, [[Shiosaki]], [[Tsukumo]], and [[Namioka]].

The {{nihongo|'''Hirogawa Iwashi'''|広川鰯|Hirogawa Iwashi}} is the prefecture's signature product, prized across Okaiken for its rich oil content and delicate flavor. The seasonal sardine runs, driven by cooling ocean temperatures from May through October, bring massive schools through the Hirogawa Strait—a natural phenomenon that has sustained the region's economy for centuries. The iwashi is processed into a variety of products including fresh fillets for the metropolitan market, {{nihongo|niboshi|煮干し}} (dried sardines for dashi stock), {{nihongo|shio-kara|塩辛}} (salted and fermented sardine guts), and canned sardines in oil that are exported across the Shendan Ocean.

Beyond iwashi, the prefecture's fishing industry harvests significant quantities of horse mackerel, mackerel, and various reef fish from the strait's rich waters. The {{nihongo|Hirogawa Central Fish Market|広川中央魚市場|Hirogawa Chūō Gyoshijō}}, located on the main harbor, handles approximately 210,000 tonnes of seafood annually, making it one of the largest fish markets on the Sea of Mitoyagi.

===Cement and Chemicals===
[[File:Hirogawa Cement Works.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Hirogawa Cement Works, one of the largest cement producers in Harujima, with the limestone quarries visible in the background.]]

The heavy industrial sector represents Hirogawa Prefecture's most significant economic diversification. The {{nihongo|'''Hirogawa Industrial Belt'''|広川工業地帯|Hirogawa Kōgyō Chitai}}, stretching along the coast from Hirogawa city to Otaira, hosts the densest concentration of cement, chemical, and steel manufacturers in southern Harujima.

The cement industry, anchored by the Hirogawa Cement Works founded in 1912, exploits the abundant limestone deposits in the Hirogawa Hills. The prefecture's cement production supplies the construction industries of Okami and the broader Harujima corridor, with annual output exceeding 8 million tonnes. Chemical manufacturers produce industrial solvents, agricultural fertilizers, and petrochemical products, while the {{nihongo|'''Otaira Shipyard'''|大平造船所|Otaira Zōsensho}} constructs commercial vessels and maintains repair facilities for the Shendan Ocean shipping fleet.

===Shipbuilding and Maritime Services===
The prefecture's shipbuilding heritage, rooted in centuries of wooden vessel construction for the fishing fleet, evolved into modern steel shipbuilding in the 20th century. The Otaira Shipyard and smaller facilities along the coast produce coastal freighters, fishing vessels, and specialized chemical tankers. The maritime services sector—including vessel repair, marine supply, and port logistics—employs approximately 22,000 workers and contributes significantly to the prefecture's export economy.
Hirogawa's pelagic fishing industry has achieved high technical integration through the Hirogawa Marine Byproduct Bio-Refinery, constructed adjacent to the central sardine and mackerel docks. The facility converts secondary fisheries catch and processing discards into high-purity fish oils, organic bio-fertilizers, and raw chitosan flakes, eliminating municipal organic waste while supplying precursors to the chemical corridor in Tokuyama. In the prefecture's highland basins, traditional papermaking families preserve cold-water washi sheet dipping, creating an economic balance between heavy coastal agro-industrial processing and mountain craft preservation.

==Transportation==
[[File:Hirogawa Station Shinkansen.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A Harujima Shinkansen Nanpu service departing Hirogawa Station.]]

Hirogawa Prefecture maintains extensive transportation connections reflecting its dual role as an industrial center and maritime gateway.

===Rail===
The prefecture is served by the [[Harujima Shinkansen]], with Hirogawa Station serving as a stop for both Nozomi and Nanpu services. The Shinkansen provides rapid access to Okami and to the southern economic centers of Anjo-Saikawa and Toyozawa. Conventional [[Okaiken Railways]] services connect the prefecture's smaller cities to the regional rail network.

===Road===
The '''Hirogawa Expressway''' runs along the coast, connecting the prefecture to the Okami metropolitan expressway network and to the industrial corridor. The highway is a critical freight route for both the fishing industry's refrigerated transport and the cement industry's bulk shipments.

===Maritime===
The Port of Hirogawa serves as a major commercial harbor on the Sea of Mitoyagi, handling both fishing fleet operations and industrial cargo. Regular ferry services connect the offshore islands to the mainland, while commercial shipping routes link the port to destinations across the Shendan Ocean.

